WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.180
Starting a process safely is only half the job.

2
00:00:04.280 --> 00:00:06.760
The script also has to interpret the result.

3
00:00:06.860 --> 00:00:14.860
A command may fail, partially succeed, produce unexpected output, hang, or return a status that requires special handling.

4
00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:18.880
The script should define what success means before later steps depend on it.

5
00:00:19.160 --> 00:00:22.280
Exit status is the basic feedback channel.

6
00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:28.940
Important commands should be checked where their result affects state, control flow, or reporting.

7
00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:33.120
Some nonzero statuses may be expected and should be handled intentionally.

8
00:00:33.220 --> 00:00:40.020
The unsafe pattern is to continue after a failed process and report misleading success at the end.

9
00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:46.780
Pipelines need attention because the most visible result may come from the final command, while an earlier command failed.

10
00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:50.880
Defensive scripts make pipeline behavior explicit when upstream failure matters.

11
00:00:50.980 --> 00:00:56.580
This prevents formatted output from hiding a failed data source, missing file, or rejected permission.

12
00:00:56.860 --> 00:01:01.580
Timeouts and retry limits are important for unattended automation.

13
00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:06.400
A process that hangs forever can block a deployment or maintenance window.

14
00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:10.980
A retry loop without limits can amplify a dependency problem.

15
00:01:11.080 --> 00:01:17.560
Safer scripts set reasonable boundaries, stop clearly, and provide enough context for operators to respond.

16
00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:20.960
Background jobs and cleanup should be deliberate.

17
00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:28.580
If a script starts work in the background, it should know who owns that process, how completion is detected, and what should happen if the parent exits early.

18
00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:38.280
Signals and cleanup behavior can be handled at a safe conceptual level: make ownership clear, avoid orphaned work, and leave systems in a known state where practical.
